9,"submitted_on_commit":"25bbdcfafa7fb4e00262868764c25af295def1ae","diff_hunk":"@@ -0,0 +1,82 @@\n+/*\n+ * Copyright Elasticsearch B.V. and/or licensed to Elasticsearch B.V. under one\n+ * or more contributor license agreements. Licensed under the \"Elastic License\n+ * 2.0\", the \"GNU Affero General Public License v3.0 only\", and the \"Server Side\n+ * Public License v 1\"; you may not use this file except in compliance with, at\n+ * your election, the \"Elastic License 2.0\", the \"GNU Affero General Public\n+ * License v3.0 only\", or the \"Server Side Public License, v 1\".\n+ */\n+\n+package org.elasticsearch.index.mapper;\n+\n+import org.apache.lucene.document.SortedDocValuesField;\n+import org.apache.lucene.search.Query;\n+import org.apache.lucene.util.BytesRef;\n+import org.elasticsearch.common.util.ByteUtils;\n+import org.elasticsearch.features.NodeFeature;\n+import org.elasticsearch.index.IndexMode;\n+import org.elasticsearch.index.IndexVersions;\n+import org.elasticsearch.index.query.SearchExecutionContext;\n+\n+import java.util.Collections;\n+\n+/**\n+ * Mapper for the {@code _ts_metric_names_hash} field.\n+ *\n+ * The field contains a hash of the field names that are a time_series_metric.\n+ * It\u0027s stored to be retrieved when reconstructing the _id field in search queries.\n+ */\n+public class TimeSeriesMetricNamesHashFieldMapper extends MetadataFieldMapper {\n+\n+    public static final String NAME \u003d \"_ts_metric_names_hash\";\n+\n+    public static final TimeSeriesMetricNamesHashFieldMapper INSTANCE \u003d new TimeSeriesMetricNamesHashFieldMapper();\n+\n+    public static final TypeParser PARSER \u003d new FixedTypeParser(c -\u003e c.getIndexSettings().getMode().timeSeriesMetricNamesHashFieldMapper());\n+    static final NodeFeature TS_METRIC_NAMES_HASH \u003d new NodeFeature(\"tsdb.ts_metric_names_hash\", true);\n+\n+    private TimeSeriesMetricNamesHashFieldMapper() {\n+        super(TimeSeriesMetricNamesHashFieldType.INSTANCE);\n+    }\n+\n+    @Override\n+    public void postParse(DocumentParserContext context) {\n+        if (context.indexSettings().getMode() \u003d\u003d IndexMode.TIME_SERIES\n+            \u0026\u0026 context.indexSettings().getIndexVersionCreated().onOrAfter(IndexVersions.TIME_SERIES_METRIC_NAMES_HASH_IN_ID)) {\n+            final RoutingPathFields routingPathFields \u003d (RoutingPathFields) context.getRoutingFields();\n+            byte[] bytes \u003d new byte[8];\n+            ByteUtils.writeLongLE(routingPathFields.buildMetricNamesHash(), bytes, 0);\n+            // Uses sorted doc values instead of numeric so that we can leverage the run-length encoding applied to ordinals\n+            context.rootDoc().add(new SortedDocValuesField(NAME, new BytesRef(bytes)));\n+        }\n+    }\n+\n+    @Override\n+    protected String contentType() {\n+        return NAME;\n+    }\n+\n+    static final class TimeSeriesMetricNamesHashFieldType extends MappedFieldType {","submitted_line":59},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]},{"groundTruth":{"original_line":112,"line":112,"comment_lines":["[][112]"],"review_comment":["Curious to see the impact on disk usage because of this. I suspect most documents with a time series block will have the same set of metrics and therefor a same hash?","I think so, too. But probably we should also sort by _ts_metric_names_hash in addition to _tsid and @timestamp, to aid with compression in case there are docs with different metrics. This would presumably also make the metric doc values more dense, as it would ensure docs with the same set of populated metric fields are stored in proximity. WDYT?","I don\u0027t think it\u0027s worth adding this to the sort config, the values will be different hashes so we won\u0027t get much by sorting. Besides, the metrics will be different.","The sort config I was thinking about is `_tsid` (asc), `_ts_metric_names_hash` (asc), `@timestamp` (desc). The metric names hash would act as a kind-of tie breaker when the `_tsid` is the same. It would sort documents with the same set of metrics in proximity to one another. This should make their doc_values more dense. It would also make run-length-encoding for `_ts_metric_names_hash` more effective. Storage-wise, the effect would be similar to having a separate `_tsid` for documents with different metrics with the benefit that we can still properly detect counter rate resets as the `_tsid` doesn\u0027t actually change.\r\n\r\n\r\n"],"path":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/TsidExtractingIdFieldMapper.java","id":35697,"created_at":"2025-01-21T10:44:55Z","original_position":46,"side":"RIGHT","position":46,"submitted_on_commit":"25bbdcfafa7fb4e00262868764c25af295def1ae","diff_hunk":"@@ -96,14 +101,18 @@ public static void createField(DocumentParserContext context, IndexRouting.Extra\n         context.doc().add(new StringField(NAME, uidEncoded, Field.Store.YES));\n     }\n \n-    public static String createId(int routingHash, BytesRef tsid, long timestamp) {\n+    public static String createId(int routingHash, BytesRef tsid, Long metricNamesHash, long timestamp) {\n         Hash128 hash \u003d new Hash128();\n         MurmurHash3.hash128(tsid.bytes, tsid.offset, tsid.length, SEED, hash);\n \n-        byte[] bytes \u003d new byte[20];\n+        byte[] bytes \u003d new byte[metricNamesHash \u003d\u003d null ? 20 : 28];\n+        int offset \u003d 0;\n         ByteUtils.writeIntLE(routingHash, bytes, 0);\n-        ByteUtils.writeLongLE(hash.h1, bytes, 4);\n-        ByteUtils.writeLongBE(timestamp, bytes, 12);   // Big Ending shrinks the inverted index by ~37%\n+        ByteUtils.writeLongLE(hash.h1, bytes, offset +\u003d 4);\n+        if (metricNamesHash !\u003d null) {","submitted_line":112},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]},{"groundTruth":{"original_line":99,"line":99,"comment_lines":["[][99]"],"review_comment":["These seem expensive.. how about using (XORing?) the `hashCode()` of each string?","This is what we\u0027re doing for the _tsid as well but with a re-used buffer, so that we don\u0027t need to allocate a separate buffer for each string in order to hash it. The benefit is that we can use a hash (murmurhash3) with a better distribution/fewer collisions compared to `hashCode`, the tradeoff is performance. But if we\u0027re not too concerned about collisions, we could also just use `TreeSet#hashCode`, or a variation that uses XOR\u0027ing or multiplying each string\u0027s hash code by 31 (similar to how `String#hashCode` works)."],"path":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/RoutingPathFields.java","id":35699,"created_at":"2025-01-21T15:10:22Z","original_position":49,"side":"RIGHT","position":49,"submitted_on_commit":"25bbdcfafa7fb4e00262868764c25af295def1ae","diff_hunk":"@@ -73,6 +78,29 @@ IndexRouting.ExtractFromSource.Builder routingBuilder() {\n         return routingBuilder;\n     }\n \n+    /**\n+     * Returns a hash of all field names that are mapped as a time_series_metric.\n+     * It\u0027s used as a component of the _id so that multiple documents with the same _tsid can exist for the same timestamp\n+     * if they contain different metrics\n+     */\n+    public long buildMetricNamesHash() {\n+        if (metricNamesHash !\u003d 0) {\n+            return metricNamesHash;\n+        }\n+        Murmur3Hasher hasher \u003d new Murmur3Hasher(SEED);\n+        int maxLength \u003d 0;\n+        for (String name : metricNames) {\n+            maxLength \u003d Math.max(UnicodeUtil.maxUTF8Length(name.length()), maxLength);\n+        }\n+        byte[] buffer \u003d new byte[maxLength];\n+        for (String name : metricNames) {\n+            int length \u003d UnicodeUtil.UTF16toUTF8(name, 0, name.length(), buffer);\n+            hasher.update(buffer, 0, length);\n+        }","submitted_line":99},"generatedCommentsNodes":[{"comment":"The `buildMetricNamesHash` method uses `UnicodeUtil.UTF16toUTF8` to convert strings to UTF-8 bytes, which is fine for encoding. However, the buffer size calculation using `UnicodeUtil.maxUTF8Length` might be overly conservative in some cases, potentially leading to unnecessary memory allocation. Consider whether a more dynamic approach could reduce memory usage without significant performance impact.","nodes":[{"id":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/RoutingPathFields.java-server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/RoutingPathFields.java--3000:3915","promptId":"#E1HWW","srcPath":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/RoutingPathFields.java","dstPath":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/index/mapper/RoutingPathFields.java","srcChanges":[],"dstChanges":[{"pos":3000,"endPos":3915,"startLine":81,"startLineOffset":4,"endLine":102,"endLineOffset":5,"length":916}]}]}]},{"groundTruth":{"original_line":172,"line":172,"comment_lines":["[][172]"],"review_comment":["Would 4 bytes suffice here? I assume these conflicts are fairly infrequent in the first place.","I think that\u0027s probably fine, yes. The question is which hashing algorithm to use that produces a 32 bit value. Let\u0027s discuss that on your comment on `RoutingPathFields`."],"path":"server/src/main/java/org/elasticsearch/common/lucene/uid/VersionsAndSeqNoResolver.java","id":35698,"created_at":"2025-01-21T14:54:36Z","original_position":13,"side":"RIGHT","position":13,"submitted_on_commit":"25bbdcfafa7fb4e00262868764c25af295def1ae","diff_hunk":"@@ -164,10 +164,14 @@ public static DocIdAndVersion timeSeriesLoadDocIdAndVersion(IndexReader reader,\n     public static DocIdAndVersion timeSeriesLoadDocIdAndVersion(IndexReader reader, BytesRef uid, String id, boolean loadSeqNo)\n         throws IOException {\n         byte[] idAsBytes \u003d Base64.getUrlDecoder().decode(id);\n-        assert idAsBytes.length \u003d\u003d 20;\n-        // id format: [4 bytes (basic hash routing fields), 8 bytes prefix of 128 murmurhash dimension fields, 8 bytes\n-        // @timestamp)\n-        long timestamp \u003d ByteUtils.readLongBE(idAsBytes, 12);\n+        int idLength \u003d idAsBytes.length;\n+        assert idLength \u003d\u003d 20 || idLength \u003d\u003d 28 : \"Expected _id field to be 20 or 28 but got \" + idLength;\n+        // id format:\n+        // 4 bytes (basic hash routing fields),\n+        // 8 bytes prefix of 128 murmurhash dimension fields,\n+        // 8 bytes prefix of 128 murmurhash time_series_metric field names,","submitted_line":172},"generatedCommentsNodes":[]}],"coveredGroundTruth":1,"uncoveredGroundTruth":3,"coveredMatchable":33,"uncoveredMatchable":26,"timing":75545,"tokensIn":26142,"tokensOut":1453}